Sidekick Codex plugin

Install:
  On first installation, validate bundled skills, fetch complete Archify and PPT Master tool resources with pnpx skills@latest in an isolated project, and assemble the plugin.
  For an existing installation, preserve plugin files; use wsley upgrade sidekick to synchronize bundled skills and upstream tool resources.
  Prepare and verify Node.js, pnpm, uv, browser and MCP packages, Chromium with Linux dependencies, and presentation Python and system tools before activation.
  Register the Wsley local marketplace and enable Sidekick when needed, including a previously disabled installation; maintain its Mira instruction block without replacing user instructions.
  Set agents.max_concurrent_threads_per_session to 20 on every install.

Upgrade:
  Skip the operation when Sidekick is not installed.
  Assemble the current bundled skills and latest upstream tool resources, synchronize system and runtime dependencies, and verify the environments before replacing the plugin.
  Refresh plugin registration and the Mira entry instruction, and enable Sidekick even if it was previously disabled.
  Update agents.max_concurrent_threads_per_session to 20 while preserving other agent settings.

Status:
  Report plugin registration, upstream tool records, managed Codex configuration, browser and MCP package versions, Chromium binding, presentation dependencies and system packages.
  Check local environments without installing, upgrading or probing external services; status skips the live browser launch test.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu, APT, AWK, an installed Codex CLI with plugin marketplace commands and a regular user with sudo access.
  Installation and upgrades require network access to the relevant GitHub, npm, Python, Chromium and APT sources.

Configuration:
  Marketplace root: ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/sidekick
  Plugin source: ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/sidekick/plugin
  Plugin registration: sidekick@wsley; installed plugin files are managed by the Codex plugin cache.
  Entry skill: sidekick:mira; describe project work naturally without selecting internal skills.
  All public skill entries and methods are maintained in assets/sidekick/skills using skills/<name>/SKILL.md; all skills use the host's default automatic selection.
  Mira owns task scope, feedback, execution state, recovery and delivery; disciplined-dev owns engineering conventions, implementation consistency and engineering validation.
  Local frontmatter contains name and description; agents/openai.yaml owns interface metadata and tool dependencies, while each skills/<name>/LICENSE retains applicable terms and upstream references with full commit hashes.
  Local skills change with the Wsley module source; installation and upgrade do not replace them with upstream instruction files.
  assets/upstream.json declares only complete version-coupled tool resources, installed under vendor/<name>; original instructions, scripts, templates and licenses remain intact.
  agent-browser supplies its current workflow through the installed CLI; the local skill loads that version-matched guide without importing a separate discovery stub.
  Resolve each tool repository HEAD once per assembly and let the skills CLI acquire the selected resources with project-scoped --copy; global skill directories are not modified.
  Follow the upstream default branch unless an explicit revision and pin_reason document a compatibility constraint.
  skills-lock.json records actual tool source commits and hashes; it is not reused to constrain later upgrades.
  Query published browser and MCP package versions with pnpm view during assembly and record them in runtime-packages.json.
  The private browser CLI and draw.io MCP use those versions; mcp.json points directly to the installed executable and ordinary use does not download or resolve packages.
  Codex configuration: ${CODEX_HOME:-$HOME/.codex}/config.toml
  Manage one <wsley-sidekick>...</wsley-sidekick> block within developer_instructions; preserve text outside that block.
  Block content: "Read and follow sidekick:mira for project work. After context compaction or a handoff, reread it and resume unfinished work."
  Set agents.max_concurrent_threads_per_session to 20 spawned-agent threads, excluding the primary thread; first installation, repeated installation and upgrades use the same value.
  Preserve other agent settings and custom roles, and all unrelated configuration.
  Use TOML Kit to preserve configuration structure and comments; reject malformed TOML, non-string instructions, invalid agent tables or limits, and incomplete or duplicate Sidekick blocks before writing.
  Back up existing targets before changes and restore plugin files and configuration if activation fails; managed configuration writes are skipped when no values change.
  Assembly or environment validation failure leaves plugin files and configuration untouched; do not fall back to older upstream versions.
  Dependency installation is cumulative like APT: completed dependency changes remain if later steps fail; rerun install to repair environments, or upgrade to synchronize module resources and dependencies.
  Managed environments: ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/sidekick/runtimes
  Browser installation uses a private agent-browser package and install --with-deps, followed by an offline headless launch test.
  Bind the validated Chromium executable at runtimes/chromium; Archify uses ARCHIFY_CHROME with that path and ARCHIFY_UPDATE_CHECK_DISABLED=1.
  Presentation installation uses uv, Python 3.12, vendor/ppt-master/requirements.txt, LibreOffice, Pandoc, FFmpeg, Fontconfig and baseline Latin and CJK fonts.
  Reuse a valid managed Python 3.12 environment; after backing up its runtime directory, rebuild an absent, damaged or incorrectly versioned environment without following environment symlinks.
  Existing managed runtime directories and the Chromium link are backed up before changes; browser and Python interpreter caches follow their tools' storage settings.
  Mira and its internal skills use installed environments directly; installation, repair and updates belong to Wsley commands rather than task execution.
  API keys, accounts, remote services and project-specific resources remain task configuration.
  Open a new Codex conversation after installation or upgrade to load the plugin and entry instruction.
